# Build your first standalone app

**Starting files:** `examples/rocci/standalone/counter/Counter.rocci`
**Verify:** GET `/` contains `#counter`; POST increment returns a
`datastar-patch-elements` event.

Canonical source: [Counter](/examples/counter/) ·
[Counter.rocci](/examples/counter/source/Counter-rocci/).
Do not copy the file into `docs/`.

This app is **one-shot**. Increment morphs `#counter` in the acting tab only.
A second tab stays stale until it clicks or reloads. That is the correct first
design. Shared streams wait until
[commands and live updates](/docs/tutorials/commands-and-live/).

## 1. State and initialization

`@context` declares the value generated `main` threads through handlers.
`@init` opens SQLite and creates the row. Failures here exit the process.

```rocci
@context { db : Sqlite.Db }

@init {
    db = Sqlite.open!(Sqlite.default_config(db_path))?
    # CREATE TABLE / INSERT OR IGNORE
    { db: db }
}
```

Components stay pure. Effects live in `@init` and handlers.

## 2. The document

`@get:view("/")` returns a full HTML document. It reads the count and calls
`counterPage`. The increment control is a real `<button>` with an unquoted
Rocci action:

```rocci
data-on:click=@post("/actions/counter/increment")
```

The card uses `id="counter"` so the later patch has a morph target. That id
is part of accessibility and of Datastar morphing. Do not restyle it as a
`<div>` with a click handler and no name.

## 3. The one-shot patch

```rocci
@post:fragment("/actions/counter/increment") = |{ db }| {
    count = increment_count!(db)?
    match Stderr.line!("counter increment count=${count.to_str()}") {
        Ok({}) => {}
        Err(_) => {}
    }
    counterCard({ count: count })
}
```

`?` on SQLite becomes a 500 if the query fails. Discard `StderrErr` so it does
not collide with SQLite's error type. There is no `@log` and no logging from
`@component`.

## 4. Run and inspect

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- run \
  examples/rocci/standalone/counter/Counter.rocci --no-window --log-handlers
```

Open the Dev panel (Performance, Source, Console) if you use the preview
window instead of `--no-window`. `Stderr.line!` appears as `source: runtime`.

## 5. HTTP verification

```sh
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/ | grep -E 'datastar.js|id="counter"|Increment'
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/actions/counter/increment
```

**Verify:** the POST body is a `datastar-patch-elements` event containing
`id="counter"`. It is not JSON.

Inspect lowering:

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- inspect \
  examples/rocci/standalone/counter/Counter.rocci
```

## Ability checklist

- Explain `@context` / `@init` / `@get:view` / `@post:fragment`
- Keep durable state in SQLite, not in Datastar signals
- Log from handlers without poisoning `?`
- Confirm a patch by HTTP, not only by looking at the window

## What Rocci did

Generated dispatch calls `init!`, serves the document, wraps the patch in one
SSE event, and closes. It did not open a live route. That is the live-counter
tutorial.

Next: [compose](/docs/tutorials/compose/),
[Update the UI](/docs/how-to/update-the-ui/), or compare with
[live-counter](/examples/live-counter/).
